1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,020
A billionaire paid $2.1
million for wine that was

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:05,690
cooked up in a kitchen.

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:11,810
A bank paid $175 million for 4
million customers who did not exist.

4
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:17,040
And a lottery jackpot worth $16.5
million was rigged by the man whose

5
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,800
job was to protect it.

6
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,090
Today, five real cons.

7
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,800
Every one of them ended in a courtroom,
and every one of them got caught

8
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:26,130
the same way.

9
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:31,360
Not by a genius detective, by one
small number that did not add up.

10
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:34,240
Stick around to the end, because once
you see the pattern, you'll start

11
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:35,930
spotting it everywhere.

12
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:43,240
Chapter 1 – The Wine That Couldn't
Exist Los Angeles, the early 2000s.

13
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:45,960
A young collector named
Rudy Kurniawan shows up at

14
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:48,350
the fanciest wine dinners in America.

15
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:52,070
He opens bottles that cost
$10,000 like their soda.

16
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:53,950
People call him a genius.

17
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:57,410
In 2006, he sells part
of his cellar at auction.

18
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:00,450
Two sales, about $35 million.

19
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:03,030
One of them breaks the world record.

20
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,480
Here's how he did it.

21
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,020
He bought old, cheaper
wine, mixed it with newer

22
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:09,970
wine, and poured it
into empty old bottles.

23
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,360
New corks, new wax, and printed labels
for the most famous wines in the

24
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:15,210
world.

25
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,790
When the FBI searched his home in 2012,

26
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,700
prosecutors called it a wine factory.

27
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:24,180
Investigators found stacks of
corks, wax, and thousands of printed

28
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:25,010
labels.

29
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:28,720
And the world's best tasters
drank it and praised it.

30
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:29,940
So what caught him?

31
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:30,970
Not taste.

32
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:31,990
A date.

33
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:36,280
In 2008, a New York auction listed
his bottles of a French wine called

34
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,230
Clos Saint-Denis with
labels going back to 1945.

35
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:44,230
The winemaker, Laurent Ponsot
saw the listing and objected.

36
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:47,430
His family didn't start
making that wine until 1982.

37
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:51,510
Those labels were 37 years
older than the wine itself.

38
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:54,120
Billionaire collector
Bill Koch told the jury he

39
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:59,490
paid $2.1 million for 219
bottles he says were fake.

40
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:02,980
In 2013, a jury convicted Kurniawan.

41
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,140
He was sentenced to 10 years in
prison and ordered to pay back more

42
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,990
than $28 million.

43
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:10,050
Chapter 2.

44
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:12,030
The Prince Who Ordered Pork.

45
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:13,040
Miami.

46
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,360
A man calls himself Prince
Khalid bin Al-Saud a

47
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:18,410
member of the Saudi royal family.

48
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:22,290
He lives on Fisher Island, one of
the richest zip codes in the country.

49
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,970
There's a sign on his
door that says Sultan.

50
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,130
He drives a Ferrari with
diplomatic license plates.

51
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:31,690
His bodyguards flash diplomatic
security service badges.

52
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:35,360
And he tells investors he can bring
hundreds of millions of dollars into

53
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:36,320
their deals.

54
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:38,490
All they have to do is get in first.

55
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,570
Investors handed him more than $8 million.

56
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:43,370
Here's the thing.

57
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:44,980
The plates were fake.

58
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,750
The badges were fake.

59
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:50,590
And the Prince was a man named Anthony
Gignac who grew up in Michigan.

60
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:52,590
And nobody checked the plates.

61
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:54,530
What cracked it was dinner.

62
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,200
A devout Saudi royal would not eat pork.

63
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:00,510
And a would-be business partner
watched the Prince eat pork.

64
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:04,470
His security team started digging
and the whole story fell apart.

65
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:05,720
Then it got worse.

66
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:09,640
Prosecutors said that since
1988, Gignac had been arrested

67
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:13,550
or convicted 11 times
for the same Prince Act.

68
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:18,350
In 2019, he was sentenced to more
than 18 years in federal prison.

69
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,120
Quick favor.

70
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:20,940
If you're enjoying these, hit like.

71
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:24,240
It tells YouTube to show this to more
people who like true stories with

72
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:25,550
real numbers.

73
00:03:25,839 --> 00:03:27,249
Chapter 3.

74
00:03:27,499 --> 00:03:29,060
The Lottery's Own Security Guy.

75
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:33,300
Every few days, millions of people
buy a lottery ticket and trust

76
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:34,080
one thing.

77
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:36,330
That the numbers are random.

78
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,900
In Iowa, the computers
that pick those numbers for

79
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:42,270
lotteries in many states
were protected by one man,

80
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,210
Eddie Tipton, the information
security director.

81
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:48,270
And he helped write the
software that picks the numbers.

82
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:52,420
Prosecutors and investigators
say he hid extra code inside it.

83
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,220
On a few specific dates a
year, the random numbers

84
00:03:55,240 --> 00:03:56,800
weren't random anymore.

85
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:58,240
He could predict them.

86
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:00,080
But lottery workers
aren't allowed to play.

87
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:03,680
So he passed the numbers to his
brother and a friend who bought the

88
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,270
tickets and collected.

89
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:08,350
Colorado, Wisconsin, Oklahoma.

90
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:11,990
Wins worth more than two and a
half million dollars combined.

91
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:14,090
Then came the big one.

92
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:16,180
December 29th, 2010.

93
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:19,520
A ticket bought at a Des Moines gas
station hits a jackpot advertised

94
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:22,390
at 16 and a half million dollars.

95
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:24,640
There was just one problem.

96
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:27,370
To claim it, someone
has to show their face.

97
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:29,970
For almost a year, nobody did.

98
00:04:29,990 --> 00:04:32,780
Then, less than two hours
before the deadline,

99
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,370
lawyers showed up with the ticket
for a mystery trust in Belize.

100
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:40,160
The lottery refused to pay until
it knew who really bought it.

101
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:42,820
Investigators released the
store's security video.

102
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:44,240
And the man buying the ticket?

103
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,420
His co-workers recognized his voice.

104
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:48,750
It was their own security director.

105
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:53,040
In 2017, Tipton pleaded guilty
and admitted he wrote code to

106
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:54,540
predict the winning numbers.

107
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,640
He was sentenced to up
to 25 years in prison.

108
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:00,540
And that 16 and a half
million dollar jackpot?

109
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:02,810
Nobody ever collected it.

110
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:03,820
Chapter 4.

111
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:06,050
The Loan That Never Ended.

112
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:10,010
Imagine your rent is due Friday
and your paycheck lands Monday.

113
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:12,210
A payday loan covers that gap.

114
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,330
Borrow a little, pay it back on payday.

115
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:18,500
Scott Tucker, a Kansas businessman,
ran online payday loans.

116
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:22,710
His paperwork said, borrow
$500, pay back $650.

117
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:24,770
Expensive, but clear.

118
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:27,110
Except that's not what happened.

119
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:30,370
The loans renewed
automatically, every payday.

120
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:34,030
Each time, the company took $150 in fees.

121
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:35,640
And the $500 you borrowed?

122
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:37,010
It didn't go down.

123
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:39,420
The Justice Department
described one borrower who

124
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:43,690
paid $1,925 on that $500 loan.

125
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:47,750
Prosecutors said the rates
were around 700% a year.

126
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:50,110
A typical credit card is about 22.

127
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:53,180
At least four and a half million
people borrowed from him.

128
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:57,600
The business brought in more
than $3.5 billion dollars.

129
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:01,090
And some of that money went into
his own professional race car team.

130
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,500
When states tried to stop
him, he claimed Native

131
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:07,590
American tribes owned the business
because states can't sue tribes.

132
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:09,850
Prosecutors called that a sham.

133
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,200
In 2017, a jury convicted
him on all 14 counts.

134
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:18,650
He was sentenced to 200 months
in prison, more than 16 years.

135
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:21,940
If you know someone who's ever taken
one of these loans, send them this

136
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:22,360
chapter.

137
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:24,710
It might save them a lot of money.

138
00:06:24,893 --> 00:06:29,260
Chapter five, four million
people who never existed.

139
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:32,550
Charlie Javice started
a website called Frank.

140
00:06:32,620 --> 00:06:36,330
It helped students fill out the
federal form for college financial aid.

141
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:39,600
By 2021, big banks wanted to buy it.

142
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:43,070
She told them Frank had four
and a quarter million users.

143
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:47,510
The SEC says the real number
was fewer than 300,000.

144
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:48,060
J.P.

145
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:50,200
Morgan did what a careful buyer does.

146
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:52,690
It asked to see the customer list.

147
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:54,800
So now she has a problem.

148
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:57,950
You can't hand over four
million people you don't have.

149
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:02,480
The SEC says she first asked her
own engineer to create fake data.

150
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:03,530
He refused.

151
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:07,580
So she and a coworker hired a data
science professor who generated

152
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:10,650
more than four million
fake customer records.

153
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:14,530
According to the bank's
lawsuit, it cost about $18,000.

154
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:15,140
J.P.

155
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:19,270
Morgan bought Frank for $175 million.

156
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:22,870
Later, the bank tried to
reach its new customers,

157
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:24,730
and the list fell apart.

158
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:29,630
In 2025, a jury convicted
Javice on all four counts.

159
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:35,100
She was sentenced to 85 months in
prison and ordered to pay back $287.5

160
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:36,070
million.

161
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:39,130
So what's the pattern?

162
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:42,040
Look at what caught
every single one of them.

163
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:45,530
A wine label that was 37
years older than the wine.

164
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:47,220
A prince who ate pork.

165
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:50,590
A jackpot nobody could claim
without showing their face.

166
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,850
A loan balance that never moved.

167
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,350
And a customer list that
was 14 times too big.

168
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:58,990
None of these were caught
by someone who was smarter.

169
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:02,640
They were caught by someone who
checked one number or one detail that

170
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:03,650
didn't fit.

171
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:05,890
So here's your three question test.

172
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:08,530
One, does this number even make sense?

173
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:11,350
Two, can I check it myself today?

174
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:14,650
Three, what happens if I say show me?

175
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:17,590
Every one of these cons
would have failed that test.

176
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:21,320
If you want more stories where the
math catches the bad guy, subscribe.

177
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:23,050
There's a new one every week.

